.TH HOSTID 1 "October 19, 2026"
.UC 5
.SH NAME
hostid \- set or print identifier of current host system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostid
[ identifier ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B hostid
command prints the identifier of the current host in hexadecimal.
This numeric value is expected to be unique across all hosts and
is commonly set to the host's Internet address.
The super-user can set the hostid by giving a hexadecimal
number, a dotted quad (a.b.c.d) or hostname.
This is usually done in the startup script /etc/netstart.
.PP
If the hostname form is used then the network interface must be \fBup\fP
so that the the resolver routines can be used OR the name must be present
in the /etc/hosts file.  The IP (dotted quad) a.b.c.d should be used if
neither of those conditions are satisfied.
.SH NOTES
If the kernel is not configured for networking 
.B hostid
prints 0x0 if 
.I identifier
is not given.
.PP
.B hostid
uses
.BR gethostbyname (3)
to look up
.IR identifier .
This results in a SIGSYS (illegal system call). The signal is caught and
.B hostid
exits quietly rather than crashing and creating a core dump.
.SH BUGS
The hexadecimal number ( 0xNNNNNNNN)
is interpreted in native not network byte order.  Remember to swap the bytes
within each short of the long.
.SH SEE ALSO
gethostid(2), sethostid(2), gethostbyname(3)
